The storage driver is offered its node by name

The photograph of run 584 says where the storage driver is.  The panel
reads

    nio: ufs probe #26 provider=cpu7 compatible="qcom,kryo"

and the NioUfs candidate carries the marks D1 and D2 but not D3 and D4.
D1 and D2 are what a probe leaves behind when it found nothing; D3 and D4
are what it leaves when it found something.  So the driver was offered a
CPU node, twenty-six times over, and said no every time.

That is not the driver's fault.  The personality that matches it says

    'IOClass'         = NioUfs;
    'IOProviderClass' = IOPlatformDevice;
    'IOProbeScore'    = 1000:32;

and nothing else.  With no name in it, the matching offers the driver every
IOPlatformDevice in the tree: the eight CPU nodes and every node under
/arm-io.  A CPU node says "qcom,kryo" and the UFS node says "qcom,ufshc",
and the driver knows only the second, so every one of them is a no.

The name is the check the matching is built for.  IORegistryEntry::compareName()
compares the node's name for exact equality and does not look at
"compatible" at all, and the personality above it -- the platform expert --
already asks for the root nub that way.  This board's tree builder writes
the UFS host controller as the node "ufs" (scripts/nio_adt.py), so that is
the string the storage personality asks for now.

Why it matters is the whole of the rest of the port.  The official boot
chain says the platform expert matches the ADT root node, the storage
driver publishes the userdata partition, the partition scheme publishes OS
and SystemOS, and the kernel matches the root volume by its UUID and
mounts it and starts launchd.  Every one of those steps after the first
depends on the storage driver being matched, and it never was: no userdata
partition, no IOMedia carrying the root volume, no root, no launchd.  The
twenty rounds of photographs that never went past the first round were all
looking at this one thing, and the waypoint never moved because the thread
that moves it was still inside the matching, offering the same driver the
same twenty-six providers.

The other personality for the same driver is left as it was: it matches
the platform expert, and the platform expert is the one that publishes the
tree's nodes in the first place.

